Using `string[]` array instances as Map keys is fragile. JavaScript `Map` uses reference equality (`===`) for object keys, so `customFontColorMap.get(someArray)` only finds an entry if it receives the *exact same array reference* that was used when inserting. This works today because D3 data-binding preserves the original data objects from `sets`, so `(e as VennData).sets` in the `.style()` callback happens to be the same reference. However, this is a hidden coupling to D3's internal data-reference behavior; any future refactor that copies or re-creates the subset objects (e.g., spreading them in `getSubsetData()`) would silently break all custom colors with no type-system warning. Replace these Maps with `Map<string, string>` keyed by `stableSetsKey(s.sets)`, which is already defined and used correctly in the layout lookup below:
```ts
const customFontColorMap = new Map<string, string>(
  sets.filter((s) => s.color).map((s) => [stableSetsKey(s.sets), s.color!])
);
// then look up with:
customFontColorMap.get(stableSetsKey((e as VennData).sets.slice().sort()))
```
